# Authors of Núcleo

Sergio Iván Ullaguari Alvarado <security@nucleoledger.com>

Who authored what, as a rule (it holds for the whole history, so it names no
commits):

- commits authored by `dependabot[bot]` are dependency updates;
- the merges of its pull requests are made by the author through GitHub's web
  interface, which records them as "Sergio Ullaguari" with a GitHub no-reply
  address;
- every other commit is the author's, under one of the two identities described
  below.

To check it against the history: `git log --format='%an <%ae>' | sort | uniq -c`
must show no identity outside these four (the two of the author, `dependabot[bot]`
and the web one); a fifth means this file has stopped being true.

Since 2026-10-02 the author commits as the identity above. Commits made before
2026-10-02 (from 2026-09-03 to 2026-09-28) record the same person under the short
name "Sergio U" and a different, personal e-mail address. The history is left as
it is: rewriting it would change every commit hash after the first one, including
those named by the published release tags.

What is signed, exactly: **commits are not**. The only commits that carry a
signature are the two kinds made on GitHub (the `dependabot[bot]` updates and the
web merges of its pull requests), and that signature is GitHub's, not the author's.
The tags `v0.3.0-alpha`, `v0.3.1-alpha`, `vsdk-0.3.0-alpha.0` and
`vsdk-0.4.0-alpha.0` are signed with the author's SSH key as the identity above
(`git tag -v`); the earlier tags `v0.1.0-alpha`, `v0.2.0-alpha` and
`vsdk-0.2.0-alpha.0` are annotated but not signed. The release binaries are
signed separately, keyless, by the release workflow (see `docs/RELEASING.md`).

Most commits also carry a `Co-Authored-By:` trailer naming an AI model (Claude,
by Anthropic), which the author used to write code and documentation. This file
records that fact as it appears in the history; it does not state what it means
for authorship or ownership.
